# vector-unified — what git carries and what it deliberately does not.
#
# This repo was created 2026-07-25 to put 5,397 lines of previously UNVERSIONED
# pipeline code and its architecture docs under history. Before that, one
# accidental delete lost the joint-embedding work outright.
#
# Rule: source and docs are tracked; large GENERATED artifacts are not, because
# they are reproducible from the pipeline and would put ~43 MB of JSON blobs into
# history with no diffability. If you later want the published artifacts
# versioned, add git-lfs and un-ignore them deliberately — do not just delete
# these lines.

# Generated joint-embedding artifacts (assets/unified.json alone is 16.7 MB)
assets/*.json
assets/**/*.json
# ...and their TIMESTAMPED BACKUPS, which the two rules above miss. A file named
# `unified.json.pre_hoops_resync_20260731_063030` does not end in `.json`, so the ignore
# never applied and a 16 MB backup of a deliberately-ignored build output became the single
# largest tracked object in an 11 MB repo. The suffix convention is shared across this
# estate — vector-equities carries 18 of them (`.pre_*`, `.fixed_seed7_*`) — so match the
# pattern rather than the one file that happened to slip through.
assets/**/*.json.*
assets/**/*.npz.*
assets/**/*.pt.*

# Acquired + intermediate data (27 MB; re-fetchable via pipeline/acquire_*.py)
data/*
# EXCEPT the hand-authored anchors. These are SOURCE, not generated output, and both
# describe themselves as "version-controlled, changed only in a commit" — which was not
# true: `data/` swallowed them, `git add -A` skips ignored paths silently, and three
# commits reported shipping changes to archetype_map.json that never entered the repo.
!data/archetype_map.json
!data/sector_map.json
# Same category, same reason. Each entry is a HUMAN judgement that a superlative on a live
# page was checked against its artifact, with the evidence written down. Losing it on a
# fresh clone would not merely lose a report — every cleared claim reverts to UNCHECKED and
# check_superlatives.py --check fails for anyone but me. `git add -A` skips ignored paths
# SILENTLY, which is how archetype_map.json was reported as shipped in three commits it
# never entered; caught here only because git printed the hint.
!data/superlative_registry.json
# 40 hand-written cross-sport analogy pairs (Brady <-> Curry, Gobert <-> van Dijk). NO
# script writes this file — grep credited analogy_triples_eval.py, but that writes
# analogy_triples_REPORT.json. It is irreplaceable human work and was ignored until now,
# while two evaluators read it as a required input.
!data/analogy_triples.json
# Symbol-level dependency hashes. Losing these on a fresh clone would not lose a report --
# it would silently RE-ADD three permanent false STALE entries and send the next reader
# into 23 training runs they do not need.
!data/symbol_dep_registry.json
# THE ABLATION TABLES AND THE SEED FLOOR. These are not "regenerable output", and that is
# now a measured fact rather than a judgement call: pipeline/ablation.py sets
# torch.manual_seed but NO determinism controls (train_stage2.py:167/170 set
# cudnn.deterministic and use_deterministic_algorithms; ablation.py sets neither), so the
# same config at the same seed produces a different result every run. Measured three times
# back to back on one box, same code: full@seed7 G2 = 0.6940, 0.6926, 0.6827. Evidence in
# data/ablation_determinism.json.
#
# An artifact that regenerates to DIFFERENT numbers cannot be regenerated. Deleting these
# destroys 23 + 15 + 3 GPU runs whose values can never be recovered, and
# check_ablation_consistency.py — registered BLOCKING in validate.py — reads the three
# ablation files and would exit 2 with "missing" on any fresh clone without them.
!data/ablation_report.json
!data/ablation_grl_seeds.json
!data/ablation_coral_vicreg_seeds.json
!data/stage2_seed_floor.json
!data/ablation_determinism.json
# Clone-vs-working-tree gate audit. Cheap to regenerate, but it is the record that 7
# checks pass here and fail on a clone, and check_cited_fields.py goes green over 0
# values there. Losing it loses the finding, not just a report.
!data/gate_inputs_tracked_audit.json
# Laptop-path audit. Tracked because laptop_paths is a BLOCKING gate and this file is what
# a reader checks to see the count is 0 rather than 0-because-nothing-was-scanned: it
# carries files_scanned and string_literals_examined for exactly that reason.
!data/laptop_paths_audit.json
# The stdout-encoding gap list. Tracked because it is a BACKLOG of 42 files, not a pass/fail
# — the value is the list surviving between sessions so it can be worked down deliberately.
!data/stdout_encoding_audit.json
# Prose-value audit. Tracked because it carries blocks_examined alongside the finding
# count — the first draft of that checker examined 6 of 60 blocks and reported 0, and
# only the denominator makes that distinguishable from a clean estate.
!data/prose_values_audit.json
# The record of four artifacts found holding a smaller sample than their own live
# page publishes. Indexed from README.md; losing it loses the finding.
!data/LOCAL_ARTIFACTS_BEHIND_PAGES.md
# The sweep log. A row is evidence a sweep happened, including when it found nothing;
# an ABSENT row is not "clean", it means nobody looked. That history is the artifact.
!data/validation_sweep_log.md
# Drift-watch history. A row is evidence the check ran, including when nothing moved;
# an absent row is not "no drift", it means nobody looked.
!data/drift_watch_log.md
# The open finding that dumbmodel.com cites two artifacts git does not carry. It is
# the record of WHY cited_fields is red; losing it on a clone leaves a failing gate
# with no explanation, which is how a real finding gets dismissed as noise.
!data/TENNIS_CITATION_GAP.md
# The late-seed scan. Cheap to regenerate, but it is the record that ONE function in
# 329 across six repos seeds after building its model — the root cause of three
# disagreeing ablation artifacts. Losing it loses the finding.
!data/seed_order_audit.json
# The four forward-probe reports. A DIFFERENT reason from the four above — these ARE
# generated, and the rule at the top of this file says generated artifacts stay out. They
# are the exception because dumbmodel.com CITES THEM BY PATH as the source of published
# numbers, and check_hub_freshness.py fails a page whose cited source does not exist
# (line 122, "cited source no longer exists"). Ignored, a fresh clone gets the citation
# without the evidence: three gate failures, and every forward-probe number on the live
# site unverifiable by anyone but me. The site's own fine print says "every number is
# recomputable from public sources", and a citation pointing at a file git never carried
# does not meet that claim.
#
# Safe to track because they are byte-DETERMINISTIC — fixed seeds, no timestamps; all four
# re-ran identical (2,872 / 3,024 / 7,265 / 8,768 bytes) before their lines were written. A
# generated file that churns each run belongs ignored, so the check is the precondition, not
# a formality — gridiron was re-run and compared before being added here, not after. If a
# future probe writes a timestamp, it does NOT get a line here.
!data/tennis_forward_report.json
!data/hoops_forward_report.json
!data/equities_forward_report.json
!data/gridiron_forward_report.json
pipeline/data/

# Scraped-page cache — 11 spotrac_nfl_*.html files at ~2.6 MB each (~28 MB).
# Caught only by checking `git diff --cached` BEFORE the first commit: staging
# had ballooned to 581,559 lines. Scrape cache is re-fetchable and belongs
# nowhere near history.
pipeline/cache/
**/cache/

# A pre-pitch-consolidation backup copy of unified.json (16.7 MB duplicate)
pipeline/data/bak_pre_pitch_con/
**/bak_pre_*/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp

# transient logs from a detached ablation re-run
ablation_rerun.log
ablation_rerun.err
grl_seeds.log
grl_seeds.err
chain_ablation.ps1
grl_paired.log
coral_vicreg.log
chain_cv.ps1
